\begin{abstract}
The Bridge of the Americas (BoA), a fixed steel truss arch bridge spanning the Pacific entrance of the Panama Canal, constitutes a singular point of failure for global maritime trade. Its collapse would obstruct the canal for an estimated 12–18 months, disrupting $\sim$5\% of worldwide seaborne trade volume. This analysis evaluates threat vectors against the BoA using a quantitative risk framework integrating structural vulnerability, threat likelihood, and consequence severity. We demonstrate that \textbf{loss-of-propulsion vessel allision} (LOPVA) presents the predominant risk, with an expected annualized loss (EAL) exceeding all other threats by 1–2 orders of magnitude. Consequently, resource allocation must prioritize LOPVA prevention through enhanced vessel traffic management (VTM) and geometric hazard mitigation over traditional security measures (e.g., explosives, cyber intrusion). This conclusion aligns with historical near-misses, engineering failure mechanics, and Panama Canal Authority (PCA) incident data.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The BoA (main span: 344 m; total length: 1,650 m) is a non-redundant, fracture-critical structure supporting two lanes of vehicular traffic and a pedestrian walkway. Its foundations rest on concrete caissons sunk to 25 m depth in the canal seabed, with the main arch transferring loads to twin reinforced concrete towers. While designed to withstand seismic events (Zone 3) and environmental loads, the bridge lacks redundancy in its primary load path—failure of a single tower or arch segment risks progressive collapse. The PCA estimates canal closure costs at \textbf{US\$200–600 million per day} in lost tolls, logistics delays, and supply chain disruption \citep{pca_econ_impact}. Thus, threat prioritization must focus exclusively on scenarios capable of inducing \textit{immediate, total structural failure}.

\section{Threat Vector Analysis}
We assess threats across four domains using the risk equation:  
\[
\mathcal{R} = \sum_{i} \left( \mathcal{L}_i \times \mathcal{V}_i \times \mathcal{C}_i \right)
\]  
where $\mathcal{L}_i$ = annual likelihood, $\mathcal{V}_i$ = structural vulnerability (0–1), $\mathcal{C}_i$ = consequence (normalized to canal closure duration). Data sources include PCA navigation logs (2018–2023), FEMA P-58 fragility models, and LTBRIDGE simulation Suite \citep{ltbridge}.

