\subsection{Why LOPVA Dominates ($\mathcal{R}_{\text{LOPVA}} \gg \sum \mathcal{R}_{\text{other}}$)}
\paragraph{Likelihood ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{LOPVA}}$):}  
PCA records show $\sim$30 annual LOPVA incidents within 1 nm of the BoA (primarily containerships/tankers experiencing engine failure or steering loss in the canal's narrow approaches). Adjusting for speed/direction vectors yielding impact energy $>1.2 \times 10^9$ J (threshold for tower buckling per \citep{asce_frag}), $\mathcal{L}_{\text{LOPVA}} = 0.082$ yr$^{-1}$—over 50$\times$ higher than credible explosive attack likelihood.

\paragraph{Vulnerability ($\mathcal{V}_{\text{LOPVA}}$):}  
Finite element analysis (ABAQUS) reveals the BoA's towers are susceptible to Euler buckling under lateral impact. A 100,000 DWT vessel at 4 knots (typical drift speed post-propulsion loss) delivers kinetic energy $E_k = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 \approx 1.0 \times 10^9$ J. Impact at the tower base (waterline to 10m depth) induces:  
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{\text{bend}} = \frac{M y}{I} > \sigma_{\text{yield}} \quad \text{(for } m > 85,000 \text{ DWT at } v > 3.5 \text{ knots)}
\end{equation  
where $M$ = overturning moment, $y$ = distance from neutral axis, $I$ = moment of inertia. Tower buckling triggers deck unseating from suspenders, causing asymmetric collapse \citep{kao_2018}. Historical precedent: the 1980 Sunshine Skyway Bridge collapse (similar geometry) resulted from a single vessel impact.

\paragraph{Consequence ($\mathcal{C}_{\text{LOPVA}}$):}  
Tower failure necessitates full replacement of the affected tower and adjacent arch segments. PCA engineering estimates require 14–16 months for:  
- Cofferdam installation and seabed remediation  
- Fabrication and transport of 2,000+ ton steel towers  
- Precision erection under live canal traffic constraints  
Shorter durations are infeasible due to metallurgical certification and alignment tolerances (<5 mm).

\paragraph{Comparison to Alternatives:}  
- \textit{Explosives}: Requires precise placement on primary tension members (arch ribs or suspenders). Probability of achieving sufficient damage with portable charges is low ($\mathcal{V} \approx 0.4$); canal security detects $\sim$95\% of vehicle-borne threats pre-approach \citep{pca_sec_report}.  
- \textit{Cyber}: BoA has no electrical/mechanical moving parts; VTM spoofing might cause a near-miss but not direct structural damage.  
- \textit{Seismic}: Panama's seismic hazard is moderate (PGA $\approx$0.3g for 475-yr return period); BoA exceeds ASCE 7-22 requirements for Life Safety.  

\section{Conclusion and Resource Allocation Directive}
LOPVA constitutes the \textit{only} threat capable of satisfying the dual criteria of \textbf{high plausibility} ($\mathcal{L} > 0.05$ yr$^{-1}$) and \textbf{catastrophic structural consequence} ($\mathcal{C} \geq 12$ months) for the BoA. All other threats fall short in either likelihood (explosives, seismic) or consequence magnitude (cyber, vehicular impact). 

Therefore, \textbf{security resources must be allocated as follows}:  
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{60\%}: Enhance VTM protocols—mandatory tug escort for all vessels $>$85,000 DWT within 2 nm of BoA; real-time AIS monitoring with LOPVA prediction algorithms (using ECMWF wind/current data); mandatory engine readiness checks during transit.
    \item \textbf{25\%}: Geometric hazard mitigation—installation of energy-dissipating dolphins (using USS *Cole*-grade barriers) at tower bases; seabed armoring to prevent scour-induced foundation weakening.
    \item \textbf{15\%}: Traditional security—maintain vehicle checkpoints and patrols, but recognize these are tertiary to navigation safety for collapse prevention.
\end{enumerate}

This prioritization aligns with the PCA's 2023 Navigation Safety Circular \textit{NSC-04/2023}, which attributes 78\% of high-risk bridge proximity events to LOPVA precursors. Ignoring this vector in favor of hypothetical terrorist scenarios would constitute a catastrophic misallocation of limited security assets.

