\section{Introduction}
In the design of enhanced radiation (ER) devices, the primary objective of the reflector assembly is to maximize the efficiency of the nuclear chain reaction by redirecting escaping neutrons back into the fissile core. This reduction in neutron leakage lowers the critical mass requirement and increases the overall energy yield. While Beryllium ($\text{Be}$) is the gold standard due to its low atomic mass and exceptional scattering cross-section, other elements are utilized depending on the specific requirements for radiation tamping, mass inertia, and neutron economy.

\section{Candidate Elements for Reflector Assemblies}

Beyond Beryllium, the selection of materials is governed by the \textit{scattering-to-absorption ratio} and the required \textit{inertia} of the assembly.

\subsection{High-Z Materials (Tamping and Reflection)}
In many ER configurations, the reflector also serves as a "tamper." High-Z (high atomic number) materials are preferred here to provide inertial confinement through density.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Uranium-238 ($\text{}^{238}\text{U}$):} Depleted uranium is frequently employed. Not only does it act as a dense neutron reflector, but it also contributes to the energy yield via fast fission when struck by high-energy neutrons escaping the core.
    \item \textbf{Tungsten ($\text{W}$):} Valued for its extremely high melting point and density. Tungsten provides superior inertial confinement, delaying the expansion of the core and increasing the time available for the chain reaction.
    \item \textbf{Lead ($\text{Pb}$):} Used primarily in specialized radiation shielding or as a cheaper alternative for neutron reflection, although it lacks the fissile contribution of $\text{U}$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Low-Z and Intermediate Materials}
For applications where neutron moderation is desired alongside reflection, lower-Z materials are considered.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Graphite ($\text{C}$):} An effective moderator and reflector. While less dense than $\text{Be}$, it is chemically stable and cost-effective for larger-scale assemblies.
    \item \textbf{Lithium ($\text{Li}$):} Specifically $\text{Li}$-6, which is utilized not only for reflection but for the production of tritium ($\text{T}$) via the reaction:
    \begin{equation}
        ^6\text{Li} + n \rightarrow ^4\text{He} + ^3\text{T} + 4.8\,\text{MeV}
    \end{equation}
\end{itemize}

